' TextsStore — the UI-language table.
'
' Phase 1 is one consumer: the Settings screen. A later wave migrates the
' remaining screens one replace-literal-with-Lang() at a time.
'
' The store is a pure lookup: data goes in through the constructor, a whole
' language slice comes back out, and no method reads a device or a registry.
' The device reads the data itself in TextsData.brs (TextsLoadData) and hands
' the parsed table in — the split keeps this class deterministic and unit-
' testable, because the brs interpreter used by `npm test` stages only .bs/
' .brs files, so the JSON a device pkg contains is not reachable there.
'
' Shapes: the data is { language: { section: { key: text } } }. A language
' whose slice is not an associative array (a malformed file, a nested bad
' branch) is dropped at construction, so every slice ForLanguage() can answer
' is one Lang() can walk. "en" is kept even when the whole table is unusable —
' the fallback language must exist or Lang() could only ever answer with keys.
class TextsStore
    sub new(data as dynamic)
        m.slices = {}
        if Type(data) = "roAssociativeArray"
            for each language in data
                slice = data[language]
                if Type(slice) = "roAssociativeArray" then m.slices[language] = slice
            end for
        end if
        if m.slices["en"] = invalid then m.slices["en"] = {}
    end sub

    ' The section maps of one language code. An unknown language answers the
    ' English slice: Lang() then paints the English text rather than a section.key
    ' placeholder — a language still missing its table must not take the UI
    ' down with it.
    function ForLanguage(language as string) as object
        if m.slices[language] <> invalid then return m.slices[language]
        return m.slices["en"]
    end function
end class
